**Proposition 5.3 (the two explicit codes have different objectives).**
At $D=3$ the preparation-optimal construction in Theorem 4.2 has
eleven attained names, with worst preparation charges $(T,L,H)=(2,4,2)$.
It is not name-optimal, because the credited [D3, Theorem 2.3][D3]
gives $M_1(3)=4$. Theorem 5.1 gives four attained names with the
simultaneous preparation guarantees $(4,12,3)$. The credited full
canonical scan had charges $(7,17,3)$.

Proof. $N_2=2+3^2=11$. Substitute $D=3$ into Theorems 4.2 and
5.1 and the scan formula in Convention 2.3; reuse the stated D3
name optimum. $\square$

This comparison proves that the displayed preparation-optimal code and
the displayed name-optimal code differ. It does not prove that no other
code can simultaneously attain the name minimum and the preparation
minima. Such a disjointness claim would require a further theorem about
the acquisition of minimum-cover names. More generally, for $D\ge2$
the new sufficient call bound is strictly below $2^D-1$, and the
letter saving relative to that scan is
$(D-2)2^{D-1}+1>0$; the peak bound remains $D$. This changes no value
of $M_1(D)$ and no memory-rate endpoint.

